{"id":"volunteer-assist","title":"Support the lead coach","minutes":6,"tracks":["volunteer"],"summary":"Be a calm, clear extra adult with an agreed role and a route for help.","sections":[{"heading":"Agree your role first","html":"<p>Your contribution begins with making the environment welcoming and manageable for each child. Before the session, ask the lead coach what you will do, where you will work and how to get their attention. Agree who checks equipment, explains the game, watches boundaries and supports children who need a starting point. This volunteer role description is a proposed application of the guides' child-centred approach, to be agreed with your programme lead.</p><p>Check current safeguarding, arrival, collection and incident procedures with that lead. This lesson does not replace them or authorise responsibilities you have not been assigned.</p>"},{"heading":"Help a child enter the game","html":"<p>Use a child's name, show where to start and keep your explanation short. If a child watches from the edge, ask what would help them join. You might show the start together or find an agreed partner. Tell the lead coach what you noticed so they can decide whether the activity needs a change.</p><p>Let children make decisions while they play. Recognise purposeful attempts with a specific comment, such as ‘You looked for a space.’ Intent over outcome is a shared value: encouragement should reach beyond children who score.</p>"},{"heading":"Keep your support connected","html":"<p>Use the lead coach's agreed explanation and restart rule. If a child needs support or challenge, share a concrete observation rather than inventing a different game across the field. Ask before changing rules or groups. Retrieve spare balls only when safe and agreed, and help children return to play without long queues.</p><p>For a safety concern, follow your briefing and alert the lead coach promptly. If a child shares a concern, listen calmly and follow the safeguarding procedure; do not promise secrecy or conduct your own investigation.</p>"},{"heading":"Reflect with the lead coach","html":"<p>Afterwards, share one observation about a child and ask for feedback on your support. What helped the child join? Did your words make the next step clear? Choose one improvement. Your pathway moves from observing, to assisting with a defined job, to leading a short agreed activity with supervision and feedback. Online completion does not establish readiness to work independently.</p>"}],"mediaIds":["child-centric"],"quiz":[{"id":"volunteer-assist-1","prompt":"The lead coach explains one restart rule, but you prefer another. What should you do?","options":["Apply your own rule.","Discuss the change with the lead coach before using it.","Tell children to choose an adult."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Consistent instructions help children understand. Agree adaptations with the session leader."},{"id":"volunteer-assist-2","prompt":"A child needs help joining. What is useful within your agreed role?","options":["Offer a clear start and share what you notice with the lead coach.","Label them difficult.","Change every rule without telling the coach."],"correct":0,"explanation":"A manageable start supports the child and stays connected to the plan."},{"id":"volunteer-assist-3","prompt":"You have read the lessons and want to lead independently. What is next?","options":["Start alone because the quiz is done.","Skip practical feedback.","Agree a supervised activity and obtain mentor feedback."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Online learning prepares you to try. Practical observation supports the next responsibility."}],"takeaway":"Agree one job, notice one child and ask the lead coach for one piece of feedback.","sourceRefs":[{"sourceId":"guide-2","page":23},{"sourceId":"guide-2","page":24},{"sourceId":"guide-2","page":27},{"sourceId":"guide-2","page":33},{"sourceId":"guide-2","page":39}]}
